WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.200
Republican Senator Mitt Romney is not someone&nbsp;
that I go to when I need advice about anything.&nbsp;

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:11.440
He's not someone that I agree with on policy&nbsp;
almost ever, except for maybe some completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:18.480
uncontroversial, small things. But he has done&nbsp;
one thing right during the last couple of years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:21.520
which is for the most part, he&nbsp;
just tells things as they are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:28.000
even when they are about the Republican Party. And&nbsp;
Mitt Romney predicted yesterday that Donald Trump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:35.040
would easily win the 2024 Republican presidential&nbsp;
nomination if indeed he tries to win it in 2024.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:41.440
And this coincides exactly with what the polling&nbsp;
has indicated that we've looked at over the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:47.280
month, which is that at least for now, and things&nbsp;
can change between now and 2022, between 20,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:54.480
22 and 2024. But for now, the vast majority&nbsp;
of the Republican Party is behind Donald Trump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.480 --> 00:01:02.240
and the 2024 nomination is his for the taking, if&nbsp;
indeed he wants it. Mitt Romney said that he is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:09.200
quote, sure, that Trump will at least play a&nbsp;
role in the Republican Party in the coming years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:15.040
pointing out that he is by far the largest&nbsp;
voice and big impact, a big impact in the party.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:22.080
And that, quote, I expect he will continue playing&nbsp;
a role. I don't know if he'll run in 2024 or not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:28.160
but if he does, I'm pretty sure he will win the&nbsp;
nomination. Romney did point out, as we've been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:33.360
pointing out, that things can change between now&nbsp;
and 2024, but that for the time being, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:39.040
I look at the polls and the polls show that among&nbsp;
the names being floated as potential contenders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:45.680
in 2024, if you put President Trump in there among&nbsp;
Republicans, he wins in a landslide. I think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:51.360
that is absolutely and completely correct. And&nbsp;
unless something changes, if Trump wants it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:55.520
the nomination is likely to be his in 2024.&nbsp;
Now, what is it that could change? And I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:58.960
this is the most important thing to think about.&nbsp;
Well, a couple of different things could change.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:03.360
Number one, Trump could change things by starting&nbsp;
his own party. And we've talked about this before.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:10.000
For now, Trump has such a following within&nbsp;
the Republican Party that if he were to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:14.800
I'm leaving the party and starting my own,&nbsp;
64 percent of Republicans say they would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:20.320
leave with him. Now, I don't believe 64 percent&nbsp;
actually would. I think when it came down to it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:25.680
particularly if a year has gone by, if two years&nbsp;
have gone by, there's a serious likelihood that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:30.320
some of that 64 percent would say, you know, I'm&nbsp;
kind of over Trump, I'm actually going to stay.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:35.360
But even if it's 20 percent or 30 percent or 40&nbsp;
percent of the Republican Party, that does leave.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:40.320
If Trump starts his own party, that could spell&nbsp;
absolute and total disaster for the Republican&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:44.000
Party. And it would also mean that the Republican&nbsp;
nominee would not be Trump because Trump would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:48.160
the nominee of whatever party has starts that&nbsp;
he starts. That's something that could change.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:53.120
The other thing that could change is that all&nbsp;
of this energy around Trump, much of it fomented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:57.600
by his claims for two months after the election,&nbsp;
that Joe Biden stole it and that he actually won&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:03.040
subsequently by continuing to claim and being&nbsp;
supported in his claims by Newsmax and 018 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:08.800
to some degree, Fox News, that he has this&nbsp;
strategy or that strategy to try to get the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:12.800
election back. That generated a lot of excitement&nbsp;
and a lot of interest and a lot of energy.&nbsp;

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:20.240
If that simply dissipates with Trump out of the&nbsp;
spotlight, then you could also see the party going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:26.160
in a different direction that maybe would not&nbsp;
choose Trump as its 2024 nominee. And then also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:32.320
I think that this will sort of be a feedback loop.&nbsp;
But if Trump sort of fades away a little bit over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:37.840
the next 18 months, it's quite possible that many&nbsp;
of the Trump and elected officials that came to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:43.120
power only because of their affiliation with Trump&nbsp;
ism. I'm thinking about Marjorie Taylor Greene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:47.280
Lauren Bobert, Madison Cawthorn, and there are&nbsp;
others. But those are sort of the big three.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:54.720
The Trumpy elected officials may get absolutely&nbsp;
destroyed in their 20 22 reelection campaigns.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.720 --> 00:04:03.120
I already saw some early data and statements out&nbsp;
of Marjorie Taylor Greene's district in Georgia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:09.920
that suggests it could be a bloodbath for&nbsp;
her in November of twenty 22. It's so long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:14.080
from now. It's very difficult to say for&nbsp;
sure. We're, you know, 19 months away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:20.000
or something like that. But if the Trumpy&nbsp;
elected officials get crushed in 2022,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:27.360
that may kind of continue this decline of Trump&nbsp;
ism within the Republican Party. But Mitt Romney&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:33.280
is completely right. As of right now, Donald&nbsp;
Trump would easily win the Republican nomination.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:38.640
And, you know, we talk about areas in which&nbsp;
Democrats and Republicans are too similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:42.160
and areas in which Democrats and&nbsp;
Republicans couldn't be more different.&nbsp;

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:48.560
Donald Trump never could have won a Democratic&nbsp;
primary in 2016, never would have happened.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:52.480
The Democratic electorate simply would not be&nbsp;
bamboozled in that way. You can. They say a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:56.160
things about elected Democrats and a lot of things&nbsp;
about the Democratic electorate, but they would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:01.440
never fall for someone like Trump. The Republican&nbsp;
electorate not only fell for it in 2016,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:07.600
they continued to support him in 2020 to&nbsp;
almost the same degree. He ultimately did lose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:13.920
the presidential election and so far they still&nbsp;
support him. That is a dramatic difference between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:17.760
the Democratic electorate and the Republican&nbsp;
electorate in terms of what they are capable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:22.640
of falling for. And at this point, all we can&nbsp;
do is wait. This Sunday, Donald Trump will make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:28.000
his first public speech since leaving office at&nbsp;
CPAC. We will be covering it live. And I'm curious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:35.440
to see if and what he says, if anything, about&nbsp;
the 2024 election or even 2022. He is expected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:41.760
to talk about the his future to some degree within&nbsp;
Republican Party politics. So that's, I believe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:48.080
in the 3:00 Eastern hour on Sunday. I'll be live&nbsp;
on YouTube, Twitter and Facebook covering that

